**Claim.** The quotient $(S^1\times S^1)/\sim$ is homeomorphic to the torus $S^1\times S^1$.

**Proof.**

Identify the torus with $\mathbb{R}^2/\mathbb{Z}^2$ via $(e^{2\pi i x}, e^{2\pi i y}) \leftrightarrow (x,y) \bmod \mathbb{Z}^2$. Under this identification, multiplication by $i = e^{2\pi i/4}$ sends
$$
(e^{2\pi i x},\, e^{2\pi i y}) \;\mapsto\; (e^{2\pi i(x+1/4)},\, e^{2\pi i(y+1/4)}),
$$
so the map $(w,z)\mapsto (iw,iz)$ is the translation
$$
T:\;(x,y)\;\mapsto\;(x+\tfrac14,\;y+\tfrac14)
$$
on $\mathbb{R}^2/\mathbb{Z}^2$.

Since $T^4(x,y) = (x+1,y+1) = (x,y)\bmod\mathbb{Z}^2$ and no smaller positive power is the identity, $T$ generates a free $\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}$-action on the torus. The quotient is therefore
$$
\mathbb{R}^2\big/\big\langle \mathbb{Z}^2,\; (\tfrac14,\tfrac14)\big\rangle.
$$

Let $\Lambda = \langle (1,0),\,(0,1),\,(\tfrac14,\tfrac14)\rangle$ be the lattice generated by $\mathbb{Z}^2$ and the translation vector. Observe
$$
(1,0) = 4\cdot(\tfrac14,\tfrac14) + (0,1),
$$
so $\Lambda$ is generated by just two vectors, e.g.
$$
\Lambda = \big\langle (\tfrac14,\tfrac14),\;(0,1)\big\rangle.
$$
Indeed $(1,0)=4(\tfrac14,\tfrac14)+(0,1)\in\Lambda$, and conversely both generators lie in $\Lambda$. The determinant is
$$
\left|\det\begin{pmatrix} 1/4 & 0 \\ 1/4 & 1\end{pmatrix}\right| = \frac14,
$$
confirming the quotient has fundamental-domain area $1/4$ (consistent with dividing the area-$1$ torus by a free action of a group of order $4$).

A lattice in $\mathbb{R}^2$ of rank $2$ yields a torus, so
$$
\mathbb{R}^2/\Lambda \;\cong\; S^1\times S^1.
$$

Hence the quotient space is again a torus. $\blacksquare$

